      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Managed care, critical pathways, and length of stay issues have a major impact on current hospital policy and patient care. In orthopedic surgery, significant strides have been made in improving efficiency, decreasing costs, and reducing length of stay. Use of vertical pathways, especially the first day of admission, the day of surgery, is important for efficient patient care. As anesthesiologists involved in the process, we must be certain that patient care is not compromised in an attempt to save money or achieve early discharge. In many studies, pain management, type of anesthesia, and amount of blood loss are not significant factors in length of hospital stay. These factors must be approached as quality-of-life issues and appropriate decisions made."},
